# A list tested in front of a loop gives `undefined` for a hole and for a position past its end alike: nothing above it holds elements. Where the one user of the read is arithmetic with a number, `undefined` is NaN there,
# and the read may give the number itself. That was said only where the position was shown to lie inside the list; a position under a mask is shown no such thing, so the turn carried `undefined` beside the integer,
# made an optional double of it, told the mark from a number and cleaned it. Now every position that is an index of a list reads so: past the end and at a hole the read gives NaN.
# It stays as it was where something can tell NaN from `undefined` (countMissing) and where the position may be no index (sumBelow): a name like "-1" is looked for as a name.
# No length is written where the compiler sees it: a call with a written length is copied, and its loop is none of these.
# f1 = sum, f2 = sumOfHalves, f3 = countMissing, f4 = sumBelow, f5 = turns
# -- source: what-lies-past-the-end-of-a-list-reads-as-nan-for-arithmetic-as-a-hole-does.ts
